      "nets": ["NET0049 (ATO_VALVE)", "+24V"],
      "observed": "In the 'ATO Valve' block the low-side switch driving the solenoid (P5 pin 2 / NET0049) is T4, a AO3400A N-MOSFET in a small SOT-23 package. No AON6407 appears anywhere on the schematic (the only other power FET, T1, is an ADN6407 used for reverse-polarity protection on the 24 V input).",
      "expected": "The specification's component table assigns the ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch to a AON6407 8-DFN power N-MOSFET: 'T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch'. The intended part for this inductive-load position is therefore the DFN AON6407, not a SOT-23 AO3400A.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve block (sheet 1, page 3 tile, region E): gate of T5 BT3904 pulled up to +3V3 via R24 10k, drain of T4 labelled 'AO3400A' tied to NET0049 -> P5 pin 2, source to GND, with D1 SS34 flyback to +24V."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch | Netlist: drives ATO_VALVE net; gate driven through R10 (100 Ω), pre-driven via T4 (BT3904)."}
      ],
      "impact": "A small-signal SOT-23 MOSFET (higher Rds(on), far lower package thermal rating) is used to switch an inductive 24 V solenoid that the design intended to be driven by a DFN power MOSFET. Under continuous valve-on or solenoid inrush the device can overheat / fail, and its switching characteristics differ from the intended part, so the ATO valve drive does not behave as specified.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic otherwise matches the specification (power train, ADM3260 isolators, TMC2209 dosing drivers, I2C pull-ups, float interlocks, buck/LDO values, and the documented known limitations), but the ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch is built with a SOT-23 AO3400A where the spec explicitly calls for an 8-DFN AON6407, and no AON6407 is present on the board — a component substitution on an inductive load."
}

